WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.920
Fourteen years of Erdoganism has transformed
Turkey from ally to adversary. What

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:12.960
does this mean for the United States?
NATO is at risk.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.440
The defensive alliance is run by
consensus, so single objection paralyzes

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.130
it. There is no mechanism to expel
wayward members. Even if Erdogan

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.269
doesn't seek NATO's destruction, Turkey
is an intelligence risk. Integrating

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.869
Russian S-400 missiles would mean
exposing NATO secrets to the Kremlin.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:32.759
It gets worse: In fits of pique, Erdogan
exposed Israeli spies to Iran, and later

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.360
leaked the location of American forces
inside Syria to the Islamic State.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.469
What comes next could be even worse: Hakan Fidan, Turkey's intelligence chief,

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:44.579
favors Tehran and Moscow over Washington.
Turkey is currently a partner on the

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:49.079
F-35 Joint Strike Fighter, America's
next-generation aircraft. Should the

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:52.800
Pentagon really trust Turkey not to
share the F-35 stealth technology with

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.879
Russia, Iran, and China? Don't even get me
started on Turkey's terror sponsorship.

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:02.280
What to do: It's time to stop pretending
Turkey is an ally. The best policies are

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.200
geared to reality, not the fantasy of how
things used to be.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.979
Do you think the United States needs a new strategy toward Turkey? Let us know in your

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:14.729
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:19.860
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.090
from AEI.

